<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Movie Is... Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Mediate between peers in conflict. Communication: Clarify misunderstood social cues in peer interactions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can mediate a simple disagreement between peers about a favorite movie, using gestures to show listening and taking turns (e.g., hand gestures for "wait" and "listen"). Can clarify misunderstood social cues, using facial expressions and gestures to show understanding (e.g., nodding to confirm a peer's idea when clarifying opinions). Can mediate discussions about movie genres, using gestures to emphasize different types (e.g., showing "big" hands for an action movie and "quiet" for a drama).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of two very different movie genres (e.g., a thrilling action movie vs. an emotional drama). Students in pairs briefly express their preference using "I prefer... over... because it's more..." The teacher then presents a short, simple recorded audio clip of two fictional students having a mild disagreement about which genre is "better" to relax. The disagreement is very slight, focused on preference, not real conflict.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to mediate the disagreement from the audio. The teacher rephrases one student's opinion ("So, Maria, what I hear you saying is you like documentaries because they help you relax?") and clarifies the other's ("And Carlos, are you suggesting action movies are more entertaining for you?"). The teacher demonstrates using gestures: a "listen" hand signal, a nod for understanding, and an open hand to encourage turn-taking. The teacher also explicitly practices the pronunciation of words like "watches" /ɪz/ and "full" /ʊ/ vs "fool" /uː/ when mediating by using example sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'mediate' a discussion?" (Expected: "To help people talk and understand each other.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'So, you mean...' what am I trying to do?" (Expected: "Clarify what someone said.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue script (text and audio) titled "Movie Night Debate" between three friends: Sofia (likes comedies), Daniel (likes action movies), and Elena (the mediator). The dialogue incorporates key vocabulary (movie, genre, character, plot, entertaining, thrilling, enjoy, relax, prefer) and grammar (present simple, comparing preferences, verb + -ing + because). Example: "Sofia: I enjoy watching comedies because they make me relax. Daniel: I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling. Elena: So, Sofia, you like watching comedies because they help you relax, and Daniel, you find action movies more thrilling. Can you explain why you find the plot of action movies engaging, Daniel?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediate between peers in conflict; Clarify misunderstood social cues in peer interactions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue and match each friend (Sofia, Daniel, Elena) to their role (Liker of comedies, Liker of action movies, Mediator).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify three phrases Elena uses to mediate the discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Sofia thinks action movies are boring. Elena helps them understand each other.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using mediation phrases to clarify opinions ("So, you mean...", "Are you suggesting...", "What I hear you saying is..."), present simple for preferences, and comparing preferences. Integrating gestures (nodding, hand for "wait/listen") and appropriate facial expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Clarification Matching:</b> Students are given sentences expressing opinions (e.g., "I like reading fiction books because they are exciting.") and a set of mediation phrases. They match the opinion with the best clarifying mediation phrase (e.g., "So, you're saying fiction books are exciting for you?"). They orally practice phrases for pronunciation of "-s" endings and vowel sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Rephrasing Relay:</b> In small groups, one student states a preference (e.g., "I prefer documentaries because they are informative."). The next student must rephrase it using a mediation phrase and a different vocabulary word (e.g., "What I hear you saying is you enjoy documentaries for their educational theme."). The third student could then add a question or comparison. This practices vocabulary like *theme, character, plot, engaging, emotional*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Genre Mediation Role-Play:</b> Students work in groups of three. Two students are given contrasting preferences for movie genres (e.g., "Student A loves action movies, Student B enjoys poetry reading"). The third student acts as a mediator. The goal is for the mediator to clarify each person's preferences and find common ground or simply ensure mutual understanding, using gestures and the target mediation phrases. The teacher provides sentence starters like "I think..." and "I like this book because..." to initiate discussion.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Books and Movies"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Mediation of Book/Movie Trailer Feedback (21st Century Project Focus).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in groups of 3-4. One student (the "Presenter") shares their prepared Book/Movie Trailer (from the 21st Century Skills Project).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student (the "Reviewer") gives feedback or expresses a mild misunderstanding about the trailer (e.g., "I think your trailer was good, but I didn't quite understand the plot. Was the character a hero or a villain?"). The reviewer must use "I think..." or "I feel..." to give an opinion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The remaining student(s) (the "Mediator(s)") facilitate the discussion. They must actively mediate by:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifying the reviewer's feedback to the presenter ("So, [Reviewer's name], you're expressing that you need more clarity on the main character's role?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Helping the presenter rephrase their trailer's content for better understanding ("Perhaps you could describe the plot more creatively to make it clearer?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using gestures (hand for "wait", nodding) and facial expressions to manage turn-taking and show understanding.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encouraging constructive feedback (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group member will rotate roles so everyone has a chance to be a Presenter, Reviewer, and Mediator.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will participate in a short, observed mediation scenario (similar to Stage 4) focusing on clarifying misunderstandings related to movie/book descriptions. The teacher will observe one or two groups directly. Students are evaluated on their mediation skills during the project feedback session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least two different mediation phrases to clarify (e.g., "What I hear you saying is...", "Could you explain what you mean?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectively uses gestures to show listening and facilitate turn-taking (e.g., nods, hand gestures for 'wait').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of social cues (e.g., acknowledging confusion, offering rephrasing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Employs target vocabulary (genre, plot, character, entertaining, emotional, prefers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces irregular present tense verbs and vowel sounds intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provides constructive feedback (Sociolinguistic).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new mediation phrase or gesture you used today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do you think you helped your classmates understand each other's opinions about their movie/book trailers?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-6 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When was it most challenging to mediate a discussion today? What could I do differently next time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can mediation skills help me in other parts of my life, not just in English class?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific mediation phrases were students able to use effectively? Which ones need more practice?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the gestures for listening and turn-taking integrated naturally by the students? How can I scaffold this further?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did the 21st Century Skills project (Book/Movie Trailer) serve as a vehicle for practicing mediation skills? Were there any groups where the 'disagreement' became too challenging?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is the final lesson in the sequence for this theme. The mediation skills practiced in this lesson are transferable and will be crucial for future collaborative projects and discussions in upcoming units, encouraging clear communication and conflict resolution in academic and real-life scenarios.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>